  "narrative": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Modern Etruscans shows early signs of reach and reuse: the book has been downloaded 1,093 times, is available as open access, and aligns strongly with Quality Education. Its record also shows funded support from KU Leuven, MDRN, and Museo Nazionale Etrusco di Villa Giulia, suggesting a collaborative scholarly and cultural context for the work.</p>\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Modern Etruscans is a 2023 scholarly book from Leuven University Press by Chiara Zampieri, Martina Piperno, and Bart Van den Bossche. The publication is openly available and has already seen meaningful download activity, which is the clearest usage signal in the current record. That pattern suggests the book is being accessed beyond its initial release, even though citation-based scholarly uptake has not yet accumulated.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The book\u2019s subject matter sits at the intersection of archaeology, art, literature, and cultural history, with concepts in the record pointing to reading, painting, visual arts, humanities, and related fields. Its abstract frames Etruria as a long-standing object of fascination for archaeologists as well as poets, novelists, painters, and sculptors, and the volume appears to examine how expanding knowledge of the Etruscans shaped modern cultural imagination. That interdisciplinary profile helps explain why the work has relevance beyond a single specialist audience.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The strongest broader signal in the record is its alignment with Quality Education, which indicates potential value for teaching and learning contexts. The work is also linked to verified funding support from KU Leuven, MDRN, and Museo Nazionale Etrusco di Villa Giulia, showing institutional backing across academic and cultural settings. In addition, the repository record identifies the book as open, which can support classroom use and wider reading access.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">At this stage, the evidence base is still early: OpenAlex records zero citations, and there is no public-event archive available in the data provided. That means the most reliable story is one of accessible scholarly reach, active downloading, and thematic relevance rather than established citation impact. The record nonetheless points to a book positioned for sustained use in scholarship, teaching, and interdisciplinary discussion.</p>",
